gcd = 5$. $461885/5 = 92377$, $230945/5 = 46189$. $T_9 = 92377/46189$.\n\n$\\gcd(92377, 46189)$: $92377 - 2\\cdot46189 = -1$. $\\gcd = 1$. $T_9 = 92377/46189$.\n\n$r_{10} = \\binom{20}{10}/(2\\cdot100) = 184756/200 = 46189/50$.\n\n$a_{10} = (46189/50)(92377/46189) = 92377/50$. Not integer. ($92377 = 50\\cdot1847 + 27$). Not integer.\n\nSo let me summarize:\n- $a_1 = 0$: not positive integer\n- $a_2 = 1$: ✓\n- $a_3 = 2$: ✓\n- $a_4 = 17/4$: no\n- $a_5 = 10$: ✓\n- $a_6 = 461/18$: no\n- $a_7 = 70$: ✓\n- $a_8 = 3217/16$: no\n- $a_9 = 5402/9$: no\n- $a_10 = 92377/50$: no\n\nSo $S = \\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}$, sum $= 2+3+5+7 = 17$.\n\nWait, let me double-check a few of these computations, especially the pattern. We got $a_2=1, a_3=2, a_5=10, a_7=70$. Interesting: $1, 2, 10, 70$. These are $\\binom{0}{0}, \\binom{2}{1}, \\binom{?}{?}$... Actually $1, 2, 10, 70$ — ratios $2, 5, 7$. Hmm, or $1=1, 2=2, 10=10, 70=70$. These look like $\\frac{1}{n}\\binom{2n-2}{n-1}$ Catalan-related? Catalan: $C_1=1, C_2=2, C_3=5, C_4=14$. Not quite. \n\n$1, 2, 10, 70$: $a_2=1, a_3=2, a_5=10, a_7=70$. Hmm $70 = \\binom{8}{4}/... $ $\\binom{8}{4}=70$. $10 = \\binom{5}{2}$. $2 = \\binom{2}{1}$. $1 = \\binom{0}{0}$. So $a_{2k}... $ no, indices are 2,3,5,7.\n\nActually $a_2 = 1 = \\binom{0}{0}$, $a_3 = 2 = \\binom{2}{1}$, $a_5 = 10 = \\binom{5}{2}$, $a_7 = 70 = \\binom{8}{4}$. The top: $0, 2, 5, 8$ — differences $2, 3, 3$. Not clean. Bottom: $0,1,2,4$. Hmm.\n\nLet me just double-check $a_7 = 70$ and $a_5 = 10$ more carefully since those are the key.\n\n$a_5$: $T_4 = 125/63$, $r_5 = 126/25$. $a_5 = (126/25)(125/63) = (126\\cdot125)/(25\\cdot63) = (2\\cdot63\\cdot125)/(25\\cdot63) = 2\\cdot125/25 = 2\\cdot5 = 10$. ✓\n\n$a_7$: $T_6 = 12005/6006$, $r_7 = 3432/98 = 1716/49$. $a_7 = (1716/49)(12005/6006)$. $1716/6006 = 2/7$ (verified). $= (2/7)(12005/49) = 24010/343$. $343\\cdot70 = 24010$. ✓\n\nLet me verify $T_6$. $T_6 = T_5 + f(6) = 461/231 + 19/6006$. $6006 = 26\\cdot231$. $461/231 = 461\\cdot26/6006 = 11986/6006$. $+19 = 12005/6006$. ✓\n\n$T_5 = T_4 + f(5) = 125/63 + 8/693$. $693 = 11\\cdot63$. $125/63 = 1375/693$. $+8 = 1383/693 = 461/231$. ✓\n\n$T_4 = T_3 + f(4) = 68/35 + 13/315$. $315 = 9\\cdot35$. $68/35 = 612/315$. $+13 = 625/315 = 125/63$. ✓\n\n$T_3 = T_2 + f(3) = 9/5 + 1/7 = 63/35 + 5/35 = 68/35$. ✓\n\n$T_2 = 4/3 + 7/15 = 20/15+7/15 = 27/15 = 9/5$. ✓\n\nGood. Now let me double check $a_4, a_6, a_8, a_9, a_{10}$ are not integers.\n\n$a_4 = 17/4$. ✓ not integer.\n$a_6 = 461/18$. $461/18$, $18\\cdot25=450$, remainder 11. Not integer. ✓\n$a_8 = 3217/16$. $16\\cdot201 = 3216$, remainder 1. Not integer. ✓\n$a_9 = 5402/9$. $9\\cdot600 = 5400$, remainder 2. Not integer. ✓\n$a_{10} = 92377/50$. $50\\cdot1847 = 92350$, remainder 27. Not integer. ✓\n\nSo $S = \\{2,3,5,7\\}$, sum $= 17$.\n\nThe answer is $\\boxed{17}$.\n\nLet me write up the proof cleanly.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786676420}
{"type": "step", "node_id": 24, "parent_node_id": 22, "role": "assistant", "content": "## Solution\n\n**Setting up the homogeneous solution.** The recurrence is\n$$(n+1)^3 a_{n+1} = 2n^2(2n+1)\\,a_n + 2(3n+1), \\quad a_1 = 0.$$\n\nThe homogeneous ratio is $\\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n} = \\frac{2n^2(2n+1)}{(n+1)^3}$. Telescoping the product from $k=1$ to $n-1$:\n$$r_n := \\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}\\frac{2k^2(2k+1)}{(k+1)^3} = \\frac{2^{n-1}\\big((n{-}1)!\\big)^2\\cdot\\frac{(2n)!}{2^n\\,n!}}{(n!)^3} = \\frac{(2n)!}{2\\,(n!)^4\\big((n{-}1)!\\big)^{-2}}.$$\nUsing $\\binom{2n}{n} = \\frac{(2n)!}{n^2\\big((n{-}1)!\\big)^2}$, this simplifies to\n$$r_n = \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2}, \\qquad r_1 = 1.$$\n\n**Variation of parameters.** Write $a_n = r_n\\,v_n$. Substituting and using $(n{+}1)^3 r_{n+1} = 2n^2(2n+1)\\,r_n$:\n$$2n^2(2n+1)\\,r_n\\,(v_{n+1}-v_n) = 2(3n+1).$$\nSince $n^2 r_n = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\binom{2n}{n}$,\n$$v_{n+1}-v_n = \\frac{2(3n+1)}{(2n+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}.$$\nWith $a_1=0=r_1 v_1$, we get $v_1=0$, so\n$$a_n = \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2}\\sum_{k=1}^{n-1}\\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}.$$\n\n**Computing the values.** Let $f(k)=\\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}$ and $T_m=\\sum_{k=1}^{m}f(k)$.\n\n| $k$ | $f(k)$ |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | $4/3$ |\n| 2 | $7/15$ |\n| 3 | $1/7$ |\n| 4 | $13/315$ |\n| 5 | $8/693$ |\n| 6 | $19/6006$ |\n| 7 | $11/12870$ |\n| 8 | $5/21879$ |\n| 9 | $14/230945$ |\n\nCumulative sums $T_m$ and $a_n = r_n\\,T_{n-1}$:\n\n- $T_1 = 4/3$, $\\;r_2 = 3/4$: $\\;a_2 = \\tfrac{3}{4}\\cdot\\tfrac{4}{3} = \\mathbf{1}$ ✓\n- $T_2 = 9/5$, $\\;r_3 = 10/9$: $\\;a_3 = \\tfrac{10}{9}\\cdot\\tfrac{9}{5} = \\mathbf{2}$ ✓\n- $T_3 = 68/35$, $\\;r_4 = 35/16$: $\\;a_4 = \\tfrac{35}{16}\\cdot\\tfrac{68}{35} = 17/4$ ✗\n- $T_4 = 125/63$, $\\;r_5 = 126/25$: $\\;a_5 = \\tfrac{126}{25}\\cdot\\tfrac{125}{63} = \\mathbf{10}$ ✓\n- $T_5 = 461/231$, $\\;r_6 = 77/6$: $\\;a_6 = \\tfrac{77}{6}\\cdot\\tfrac{461}{231} = 461/18$ ✗\n- $T_6 = 12005/6006$, $\\;r_7 = 1716/49$: $\\;a_7 = \\tfrac{1716}{49}\\cdot\\tfrac{12005}{6006} = \\tfrac{2}{7}\\cdot\\tfrac{12005}{49} = \\tfrac{24010}{343} = \\mathbf{70}$ ✓\n- $T_7 = 12868/6435$, $\\;r_8 = 6435/64$: $\\;a_8 = \\tfrac{6435}{64}\\cdot\\tfrac{12868}{6435} = 12868/64 = 3217/16$ ✗\n- $T_8 = 24309/12155$, $\\;r_9 = 24310/81$: $\\;a_9 = \\tfrac{24310}{81}\\cdot\\tfrac{24309}{12155} = \\tfrac{2\\cdot24309}{81} = 48618/81 = 5402/9$ ✗\n- $T_9 = 92377/46189$, $\\;r_{10} = 46189/50$: $\\;a_{10} = \\tfrac{46189}{50}\\cdot\\tfrac{92377}{46189} = 92377/50$ ✗\n\n(For $n=1$: $a_1=0$, not positive.)\n\n**Verification of non-integers:** $17/4$, $461/18$ (rem 11), $3217/16$ (rem 1), $5402/9$ (rem 2), $92377/50$ (rem 27) — none are integers.\n\n**Conclusion.** The set of indices $n\\in\\{1,\\dots,10\\}$ with $a_n$ a positive integer is $S=\\{2,3,5,7\\}$, and\n$$\\sum_{n\\in S} n = 2+3+5+7 = \\boxed{17}.$$\n\n### PROOF COMPLETE", "thinking": "Let me solve this problem. The sequence is defined by $a_1 = 0$ and $(n+1)^3 a_{n+1} = 2n^2(2n+1) a_n + 2(3n+1)$ for $n \\geq 1$. I need to find which $a_n$ for $n \\in \\{1,...,10\\}$ are positive integers, and sum those indices.\n\nLet me compute the recursion. Let me try to find a closed form or just compute.\n\nLet me try $b_n = a_n / c_n$ for some normalizing factor. The homogeneous part: $(n+1)^3 a_{n+1} = 2n^2(2n+1) a_n$, so $a_{n+1}/a_n = 2n^2(2n+1)/(n+1)^3$.\n\nTelescoping: $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{2k^2(2k+1)}{(k+1)^3}$.\n\nLet me compute: $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} 2k^2(2k+1)/(k+1)^3 = \\frac{2^{n-1} \\prod k^2 \\prod (2k+1)}{\\prod (k+1)^3}$.\n\n$\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} k^2 = ((n-1)!)^2$. $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}(k+1)^3 = (n!)^3/1^3... = (n!)^3$. Actually $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}(k+1) = 2\\cdot3\\cdots n = n!$, so cubed is $(n!)^3$.\n\n$\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1}(2k+1) = 3\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdots(2n-1) = \\frac{(2n)!}{2^n n!} / 1 = \\frac{(2n)!}{2^n n!}$. Wait, $1\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdots(2n-1) = \\frac{(2n)!}{2^n n!}$. So $3\\cdot5\\cdots(2n-1) = \\frac{(2n)!}{2^n n!}$ (since we start from 1, product from k=1 to n-1 of (2k+1) = 3·5·...·(2n-1), and 1·3·5·...·(2n-1) = (2n)!/(2^n n!), so dividing by 1 gives same). Actually product k=1 to n-1 of (2k+1) = 3·5·...·(2n-1) = (2n)!/(2^n n!)/1 = (2n)!/(2^n n!).\n\nSo homogeneous ratio $r_n = a_n^{(hom)}/a_1$ factor: $\\prod_{k=1}^{n-1} = \\frac{2^{n-1} ((n-1)!)^2 \\cdot (2n)!/(2^n n!)}{(n!)^3} = \\frac{2^{n-1} (n-1)!^2 (2n)!}{2^n n! (n!)^3} = \\frac{(n-1)!^2 (2n)!}{2 n! (n!)^3}$.\n\n$n! = n(n-1)!$, so $(n-1)!^2/n! = (n-1)!/n$. And $(n!)^3$ in denominator... let me redo:\n\n$r_n = \\frac{2^{n-1} ((n-1)!)^2 \\cdot (2n)!/(2^n n!)}{(n!)^3} = \\frac{((n-1)!)^2 (2n)!}{2 \\cdot n! \\cdot (n!)^3} = \\frac{((n-1)!)^2 (2n)!}{2 (n!)^4}$.\n\n$((n-1)!)^2/(n!)^4 = ((n-1)!)^2/(n^4 ((n-1)!)^4) = 1/(n^4 ((n-1)!)^2)$. So $r_n = \\frac{(2n)!}{2 n^4 ((n-1)!)^2} = \\frac{(2n)!}{2 n^4 ((n-1)!)^2}$.\n\nNote $\\binom{2n}{n} = (2n)!/(n!)^2 = (2n)!/(n^2 ((n-1)!)^2)$. So $r_n = \\frac{n^2 \\binom{2n}{n}}{2 n^4} = \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2}$.\n\nSo the homogeneous solution is $a_n = C \\cdot \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2}$.\n\nNow use variation of parameters / particular solution. The recurrence:\n$(n+1)^3 a_{n+1} - 2n^2(2n+1) a_n = 2(3n+1)$.\n\nLet $a_n = \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2} u_n$. Then plug in. Actually let me use the standard method: let $a_n = r_n \\cdot v_n$ where $r_n = \\binom{2n}{n}/(2n^2)$ is the homogeneous solution with $r_1 = \\binom{2}{1}/2 = 1$.\n\nThen $(n+1)^3 r_{n+1} v_{n+1} - 2n^2(2n+1) r_n v_n = 2(3n+1)$.\n\nSince $r_{n+1}/r_n = 2n^2(2n+1)/(n+1)^3$ (homogeneous ratio), we have $(n+1)^3 r_{n+1} = 2n^2(2n+1) r_n$. So:\n\n$2n^2(2n+1) r_n (v_{n+1} - v_n) = 2(3n+1)$.\n\n$v_{n+1} - v_n = \\frac{2(3n+1)}{2n^2(2n+1) r_n} = \\frac{3n+1}{n^2(2n+1) r_n}$.\n\n$r_n = \\binom{2n}{n}/(2n^2)$, so $n^2 r_n = \\binom{2n}{n}/2$. Thus:\n\n$v_{n+1} - v_n = \\frac{3n+1}{(2n+1) \\binom{2n}{n}/2} = \\frac{2(3n+1)}{(2n+1)\\binom{2n}{n}}$.\n\nSo $v_n = v_1 + \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}$.\n\nWith $a_1 = 0 = r_1 v_1 = 1 \\cdot v_1$, so $v_1 = 0$.\n\nThus $a_n = r_n \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}} = \\frac{\\binom{2n}{n}}{2n^2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}$.\n\nNow I need to evaluate the sum $T_m = \\sum_{k=1}^{m} \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}$ where $m = n-1$.\n\nLet me compute the term $f(k) = \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}}$.\n\nLet me try to find a telescoping form. We want to write $f(k) = g(k) - g(k+1)$ or similar, where $g$ involves $\\binom{2k}{k}$.\n\nNote $\\binom{2k}{k} = \\frac{(2k)!}{(k!)^2}$. And $\\frac{1}{\\binom{2k}{k}} = \\frac{(k!)^2}{(2k)!} = \\frac{k+1}{2k+1} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\binom{2k+1}{k}}... $ hmm.\n\nActually $\\frac{1}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}} = \\frac{1}{(2k+1)} \\cdot \\frac{(k!)^2}{(2k)!} = \\frac{(k!)^2}{(2k+1)!} = \\frac{1}{\\binom{2k+1}{k} \\cdot (k+1)}... $ let me check: $\\binom{2k+1}{k} = \\frac{(2k+1)!}{k!(k+1)!}$, so $\\frac{1}{\\binom{2k+1}{k}} = \\frac{k!(k+1)!}{(2k+1)!} = \\frac{(k!)^2 (k+1)}{(2k+1)!}$. So $\\frac{(k!)^2}{(2k+1)!} = \\frac{1}{(k+1)\\binom{2k+1}{k}}$.\n\nThus $\\frac{1}{(2k+1)\\binom{2k}{k}} = \\frac{1}{(k+1)\\binom{2k+1}{k}}$.\n\nSo $f(k) = \\frac{2(3k+1)}{(k+1)\\binom{2k+1}{k}}$.\n\nHmm, let me try another approach. Consider $\\frac{4^k}{\\binom{2k}{k}}$ type things. Actually let me just compute numerically.\n\nLet me compute $f(k)$ for small $k$:\n- $k=1$: $\\frac{2\\cdot4}{3\\cdot2} = 8/6 = 4/3$.\n- $k=2$: $\\frac{2\\cdot7}{5\\cdot6} = 14/30 = 7/15$.\n- $k=3$: $\\frac{2\\cdot10}{7\\cdot20} = 20/140 = 1/7$.\n- $k=4$: $\\frac{2\\cdot13}{9\\cdot70} = 26/630 = 13/315$.\n- $k=5$: $\\frac{2\\cdot16}{11\\cdot252} = 32/2772 = 8/693$.\n- $k=6$: $\\frac{2\\cdot19}{13\\cdot924} = 38/12012 = 19/6006$.\n- $k=7$: $\\frac{2\\cdot22}{15\\cdot3432} = 44/51480 = 11/12870$.\n- $k=8$: $\\frac{2\\cdot25}{17\\cdot12870} = 50/218790 = 5/21879$.\n- $k=9$: $\\frac{2\\cdot28}{19\\cdot48620} = 56/923780 = 14/230945$.\n\nNow $T_m = \\sum_{k=1}^m f(k)$, $a_n = r_n T_{n-1}$, $r_n = \\binom{2n}{n}/(2n^2)$.\n\nLet me compute $a_n$ for $n=1$ to $10$.\n\n$n=1$: $a_1 = 0$. Not positive integer (it's 0, not positive).\n\n$n=2$: $T_1 = 4/3$. $r_2 = \\binom{4}{2}/(2\\cdot4) = 6/8 = 3/4$. $a_2 = (3/4)(4/3) = 1$. Positive integer! ✓\n\n$n=3$: $T_2 = 4/3 + 7/15 = 20/15 + 7/15 = 27/15 = 9/5$. $r_3 = \\binom{6}{3}/(2\\cdot9) = 20/18 = 10/9$. $a_3 = (10/9)(9/5) = 2$. Positive integer! ✓\n\n$n=4$: $T_3 = 9/5 + 1/7 = 63/35 + 5/35 = 68/35$. $r_4 = \\binom{8}{4}/(2\\cdot16) = 70/32 = 35/16$. $a_4 = (35/16)(68/35) = 68/16 = 17/4$. Not integer.\n\n$n=5$: $T_4 = 68/35 + 13/315 = 612/315 + 13/315 = 625/315 = 125/63$. $r_5 = \\binom{10}{5}/(2\\cdot25) = 252/50 = 126/25$. $a_5 = (126/25)(125/63) = (126\\cdot125)/(25\\cdot63) = (126\\cdot5)/(63) = 630/63 = 10$. Positive integer! ✓\n\n$n=6$: $T_5 = 125/63 + 8/693 = 1375/693 + 8/693 = 1383/693 = 461/231$. $r_6 = \\binom{12}{6}/(2\\cdot36) = 924/72 = 77/6$. $a_6 = (77/6)(461/231) = (77\\cdot461)/(6\\cdot231) = (77\\cdot461)/1386$. $1386 = 6\\cdot231 = 6\\cdot231$. $77/231 = 1/3$. So $a_6 = 461/(6\\cdot3) = 461/18$. Not integer.\n\nWait let me recheck: $77/231 = 1/3$? $231 = 3\\cdot77$. Yes. So $a_6 = (1/3)(461/6) = 461/18$. $461 = 18\\cdot25 + 11 = 450+11$, so $461/18$ not integer. Not integer.\n\n$n=7$: $T_6 = 461/231 + 19/6006$. $6006 = 26\\cdot231$. So $461/231 = 461\\cdot26/6006 = 11986/6006$. $T_6 = (11986+19)/6006 = 12005/6006$. $r_7 = \\binom{14}{7}/(2\\cdot49) = 3432/98 = 1716/49$. $a_7 = (1716/49)(12005/6006)$. \n\n$1716/6006$: $\\gcd$? $6006 = 3.5\\cdot1716... $ $1716\\cdot3 = 5148$, $6006-5148=858$, $1716 = 2\\cdot858$, so $\\gcd=858$. $1716/858=2$, $6006/858=7$. So $1716/6006 = 2/7$.\n\n$a_7 = (2/7)(12005/49) = 24010/343$. $343 = 7^3$. $24010/343$: $343\\cdot70 = 24010$. Yes! $343\\cdot70 = 24010$. So $a_7 = 70$. Positive integer! ✓\n\n$n=8$: $T_7 = 12005/6006 + 11/12870$. Need common denominator. $\\gcd(6006,12870)$. $12870 = 2\\cdot6006 + 858$. $6006 = 6\\cdot858 + 858... $ $6006 = 7\\cdot858$, $12870 = 15\\cdot858$. So $\\gcd = 858$. $6006/858 = 7$, $12870/858 = 15$. LCM $= 858\\cdot7\\cdot15 = 858\\cdot105 = 90090$.\n\n$12005/6006 = 12005\\cdot15/90090 = 180075/90090$. $11/12870 = 11\\cdot7/90090 = 77/90090$. $T_7 = 180152/90090 = 90076/45045$.\n\n$r_8 = \\binom{16}{8}/(2\\cdot64) = 12870/128 = 6435/64$.\n\n$a_8 = (6435/64)(90076/45045)$. $6435/45045 = 1/7$ (since $45045 = 7\\cdot6435$). So $a_8 = (1/7)(90076/64) = 90076/448$. $448 = 7\\cdot64$. $90076/448$: $448\\cdot200 = 89600$, $90076-89600=476$, $476/448$ not integer. So $a_8 = 90076/448$. Not integer.\n\nHmm wait let me double check $T_7$. Actually let me recompute more carefully.\n\nActually let me recompute $f(7) = 11/12870$. And $T_6 = 12005/6006$. \n\n$12005/6006 + 11/12870$. LCM(6006,12870). $6006 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot7\\cdot11\\cdot13$. $12870 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot... $ $12870/2 = 6435$, $6435/3=2145$, $2145/5=429$, $429=3\\cdot143=3\\cdot11\\cdot13$. So $12870 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot3\\cdot11\\cdot13 = 2\\cdot9\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13$? Wait $12870 = 2\\cdot6435 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot2145 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot429 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot5\\cdot3\\cdot143 = 2\\cdot3^2\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13$.\n\n$6006 = 2\\cdot3003 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot1001 = 2\\cdot3\\cdot7\\cdot11\\cdot13$.\n\nLCM $= 2\\cdot3^2\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdot11\\cdot13 = 2\\cdot9\\cdot5\\cdot7\\cdot11\\cdot13 = 90090$. Good.\n\n$12005/6006 = 12005\\cdot15/90090 = 180075/90090$. $11/12870 = 11\\cdot7/90090 = 77/90090$. Sum $= 180152/90090$. Divide by 2: $90076/45045$. \n\n$\\gcd(90076, 45045)$? $45045$ is odd. $90076$ is even. $\\gcd$ odd. $90076 = 2\\cdot45038$. $45045$... $90076 - 2\\cdot45045 = 90076 - 90090 = -14$. So $\\gcd(45045, 14)$. $45045 = 3217\\cdot14 + 7$, $14 = 2\\cdot7$. So $\\gcd = 7$. $90076/7 = 12868$, $45045/7 = 6435$. So $T_7 = 12868/6435$.\n\nLet me redo: $180152/90090$, divide by 2 = $90076/45045$, divide by 7 = $12868/6435$. Check $12868\\cdot7 = 90076$? $12868\\cdot7 = 90076$. Yes. $6435\\cdot7 = 45045$. Yes.\n\nSo $T_7 = 12868/6435$.\n\n$r_8 = 12870/128 = 6435/64$.\n\n$a_8 = (6435/64)(12868/6435) = 12868/64 = 3217/16$. Not integer. ($3217 = 16\\cdot201 + 1$). Not integer.\n\n$n=9$: $T_8 = T_7 + f(8) = 12868/6435 + 5/21879$. \n\n$21879 = ?$. $21879/3 = 7293$, $7293/3 = 2431$, $2431 = 11\\cdot221 = 11\\cdot13\\cdot17$. So $21879 = 9\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17 = 3^2\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17$.\n\n$6435 = 3^2\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot... $ wait $6435 = 5\\cdot1287 = 5\\cdot3\\cdot429 = 5\\cdot3\\cdot3\\cdot143 = 3^2\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13$.\n\nLCM$(6435, 21879) = 3^2\\cdot5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17 = 6435\\cdot17 = 109395$. And $21879\\cdot5 = 109395$. Good.\n\n$12868/6435 = 12868\\cdot17/109395 = 218756/109395$. $5/21879 = 5\\cdot5/109395 = 25/109395$. $T_8 = 218781/109395$.\n\n$\\gcd(218781, 109395)$? $218781 - 2\\cdot109395 = 218781 - 218790 = -9$. So $\\gcd(109395, 9)$. $109395/9 = 12155$. So $\\gcd = 9$. $218781/9 = 24309$, $109395/9 = 12155$. $T_8 = 24309/12155$.\n\nCheck $\\gcd(24309, 12155)$: $24309 - 2\\cdot12155 = -1$. So $\\gcd = 1$. $T_8 = 24309/12155$.\n\n$r_9 = \\binom{18}{9}/(2\\cdot81) = 48620/162 = 24310/81$.\n\n$a_9 = (24310/81)(24309/12155)$. $24310/12155 = 2$? $12155\\cdot2 = 24310$. Yes! So $a_9 = 2\\cdot24309/81 = 48618/81$. $48618/81$: $81\\cdot600 = 48600$, $48618-48600=18$, $18/81 = 2/9$. So $a_9 = 600 + 2/9 = 5402/9$. Not integer.\n\nHmm, $48618/81$. $48618 = 81\\cdot600 + 18$. $81\\cdot600 = 48600$. So $48618/81 = 600\\frac{18}{81} = 600\\frac{2}{9}$. Not integer.\n\n$n=10$: $T_9 = T_8 + f(9) = 24309/12155 + 14/230945$.\n\n$230945 = ?$. $230945/5 = 46189$. $46189/11 = 4199$. $4199/13 = 323$. $323 = 17\\cdot19$. So $230945 = 5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17\\cdot19$.\n\n$12155 = 5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17$ (from before: $12155 = 5\\cdot2431 = 5\\cdot11\\cdot13\\cdot17$).\n\nSo $230945 = 12155\\cdot19$. \n\n$24309/12155 = 24309\\cdot19/230945 = 461871/230945$. $14/230945$. $T_9 = 461885/230945$.\n\n$\\gcd(461885, 230945)$? $461885 - 2\\cdot230945 = -5$. So $\\gcd(230945, 5)$. $230945/5 = 46189$. So $\\gcd = 5$. $461885/5 = 92377$, $230945/5 = 46189$. $T_9 = 92377/46189$.\n\n$\\gcd(92377, 46189)$: $92377 - 2\\cdot46189 = -1$. $\\gcd = 1$. $T_9 = 92377/46189$.\n\n$r_{10} = \\binom{20}{10}/(2\\cdot100) = 184756/200 = 46189/50$.\n\n$a_{10} = (46189/50)(92377/46189) = 92377/50$. Not integer. ($92377 = 50\\cdot1847 + 27$). Not integer.\n\nSo let me summarize:\n- $a_1 = 0$: not positive integer\n- $a_2 = 1$: ✓\n- $a_3 = 2$: ✓\n- $a_4 = 17/4$: no\n- $a_5 = 10$: ✓\n- $a_6 = 461/18$: no\n- $a_7 = 70$: ✓\n- $a_8 = 3217/16$: no\n- $a_9 = 5402/9$: no\n- $a_10 = 92377/50$: no\n\nSo $S = \\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}$, sum $= 2+3+5+7 = 17$.\n\nWait, let me double-check a few of these computations, especially the pattern. We got $a_2=1, a_3=2, a_5=10, a_7=70$. Interesting: $1, 2, 10, 70$. These are $\\binom{0}{0}, \\binom{2}{1}, \\binom{?}{?}$... Actually $1, 2, 10, 70$ — ratios $2, 5, 7$. Hmm, or $1=1, 2=2, 10=10, 70=70$. These look like $\\frac{1}{n}\\binom{2n-2}{n-1}$ Catalan-related? Catalan: $C_1=1, C_2=2, C_3=5, C_4=14$. Not quite. \n\n$1, 2, 10, 70$: $a_2=1, a_3=2, a_5=10, a_7=70$. Hmm $70 = \\binom{8}{4}/... $ $\\binom{8}{4}=70$. $10 = \\binom{5}{2}$. $2 = \\binom{2}{1}$. $1 = \\binom{0}{0}$. So $a_{2k}... $ no, indices are 2,3,5,7.\n\nActually $a_2 = 1 = \\binom{0}{0}$, $a_3 = 2 = \\binom{2}{1}$, $a_5 = 10 = \\binom{5}{2}$, $a_7 = 70 = \\binom{8}{4}$. The top: $0, 2, 5, 8$ — differences $2, 3, 3$. Not clean. Bottom: $0,1,2,4$. Hmm.\n\nLet me just double-check $a_7 = 70$ and $a_5 = 10$ more carefully since those are the key.\n\n$a_5$: $T_4 = 125/63$, $r_5 = 126/25$. $a_5 = (126/25)(125/63) = (126\\cdot125)/(25\\cdot63) = (2\\cdot63\\cdot125)/(25\\cdot63) = 2\\cdot125/25 = 2\\cdot5 = 10$. ✓\n\n$a_7$: $T_6 = 12005/6006$, $r_7 = 3432/98 = 1716/49$. $a_7 = (1716/49)(12005/6006)$. $1716/6006 = 2/7$ (verified). $= (2/7)(12005/49) = 24010/343$. $343\\cdot70 = 24010$. ✓\n\nLet me verify $T_6$. $T_6 = T_5 + f(6) = 461/231 + 19/6006$. $6006 = 26\\cdot231$. $461/231 = 461\\cdot26/6006 = 11986/6006$. $+19 = 12005/6006$. ✓\n\n$T_5 = T_4 + f(5) = 125/63 + 8/693$. $693 = 11\\cdot63$. $125/63 = 1375/693$. $+8 = 1383/693 = 461/231$. ✓\n\n$T_4 = T_3 + f(4) = 68/35 + 13/315$. $315 = 9\\cdot35$. $68/35 = 612/315$. $+13 = 625/315 = 125/63$. ✓\n\n$T_3 = T_2 + f(3) = 9/5 + 1/7 = 63/35 + 5/35 = 68/35$. ✓\n\n$T_2 = 4/3 + 7/15 = 20/15+7/15 = 27/15 = 9/5$. ✓\n\nGood. Now let me double check $a_4, a_6, a_8, a_9, a_{10}$ are not integers.\n\n$a_4 = 17/4$. ✓ not integer.\n$a_6 = 461/18$. $461/18$, $18\\cdot25=450$, remainder 11. Not integer. ✓\n$a_8 = 3217/16$. $16\\cdot201 = 3216$, remainder 1. Not integer. ✓\n$a_9 = 5402/9$. $9\\cdot600 = 5400$, remainder 2. Not integer. ✓\n$a_{10} = 92377/50$. $50\\cdot1847 = 92350$, remainder 27. Not integer. ✓\n\nSo $S = \\{2,3,5,7\\}$, sum $= 17$.\n\nThe answer is $\\boxed{17}$.\n\nLet me write up the proof cleanly.", "tool_calls": [], "created_at": 1786676420}
